{
  "title": "Coupling-modulated ring (MZI coupler)",
  "group": "Lasers & modulators",
  "description": "Coupling-modulated silicon microring built from parts: two 50/50 directional couplers and push-pull PN-junction phase shifters form an MZI coupler, so 0-4 V of drive swings the ring from over- to under-coupled (notch -11 dB -> null -> -7 dB, linewidth 39 -> 18 pm) with the resonance fixed to within 3 pm. Waveguides are steady-state S-matrices, so this is a spectral (DC sweep) testbench",
  "schematic": {
    "notes": [
      {
        "x": 40,
        "y": -300,
        "title": "Coupling-modulated ring \u2014 what this testbench does",
        "lines": [
          "A microring whose bus coupler is itself an MZI: 50/50",
          "couplers DC1 and DC2 bound two arms, each with a 400 um",
          "depletion PN phase shifter (PN_A on the bus side, PN_B",
          "on the ring side). WG_R1 + WG_R2 close the ring.",
          "The arm phase difference sets the effective coupling,",
          "k_eff^2 = cos^2(dphi/2). WG_A is 209 nm longer than WG_B,",
          "parking the ring at critical coupling at mid-bias (a",
          "heater does this on a real chip).",
          "Series push-pull: PN_A sees V_R = VDRV, PN_B sees",
          "V_R = 4 V - VDRV. Stepping VDRV 0..4 V takes the ring from",
          "over-coupled (broad, -11 dB) through critical (null) to",
          "under-coupled (narrow, -7 dB) while the resonance stays",
          "within 3 pm: the junctions modulate the coupling, not",
          "the round-trip phase. Probe ring (hidden) is the",
          "intracavity power: untick its Hide from plots to see it.",
          "",
          "Set DC1.coupling = DC2.coupling = 0.3 and re-run: the",
          "drive now leaks into the ring phase, so the notch walks",
          "~12 pm and never fully nulls."
        ]
      }
    ],
